  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + z²) / 2, drawn at 0.4 × loss. x is the steep direction, so steps overshoot when learning rate > 0.4 and swings grow (diverge) when it is > 0.8.",
    "Each step: move by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per setting is Gaussian with sd 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: 1.5 at b=1, exactly 0 at b=64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (learning rate × slope), drawn at 3× their true length, one scale for both. Purple: the noisy step the ball will take. Dark: the full-data step, against the true slope.",
    "Diverged means the ball left the drawn bowl (|x| or |z| > 2.5); the run stops, the ball and path are held at the rim, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished). Noise comes from a fixed sequence: Reset replays it from the start; a new run continues with the next values.",
    "With noise a step lowers the loss only on average; single steps can raise it, especially with small batches."
  ]
}